  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nMid level heights are gradually building east this afternoon as the \nexpansive ridge is progged to shift towards the southeast CONUS this \nweekend. Isolated storm chances are below 10% this afternoon given \nthe stout 700 mb capping inversion, suppressing diurnal convection. \nNorthern stream upper trough traversing the northern plains should \ngenerate another MCS eastward and away from the region overnight \ntonight. \n\nAs the sfc ridge axis slides eastward, lee troughing builds over \neastern CO, increasing southerly winds Saturday afternoon from 15 to \n20 mph with gusts to near 30 mph. Gusty sfc winds persist Sunday and \nMonday as mid level southwesterlies increase, enhancing warm air \nadvection. Decent boundary layer mixing should lower dewpoints into \nthe low and middle 60s as high temperatures reach the mid to upper \n90s. Peak heat indices as a result hover in the low 100s through at \nleast next Thursday for portions of eastern Kansas. Warm low records \nare at stake Sunday-Tuesday with forecast lows in the upper 70s. \nMore uncertainty in temps exist towards central Kansas as a weak \nfront approaches from the northwest beginning Sunday evening. Model \nensembles are trending drier for Sunday evening through Tuesday as \nthe front is further displaced west along with the more organized \nlift. Late in the forecast period (Wednesday-Thursday), residual \ncloud cover from overnight convection may signal slightly lower \nhighs over northern Kansas.",
